 the the blue  curve such that there's say only five  percent of the areas under this left  tail  and to do that we need to somehow  estimate the blue curve  well if we don't have any data points  what can we do  well one possibility is we might have  access to unlabeled data that is  contaminated so let's say that we have a  training sample s naught of d of clean  data from the nominal distribution  and then we have an unlabeled sample s m  which is a mixture  with uh  probability fraction 1 minus alpha are  coming from the nominal distribution and  some fractions some proportion alpha are  anomalies so we're saying we have alpha  percent contamination in this mixture  okay and we'll come back to how we might  know what alpha is but uh but uh if we  assume we know alpha  then um if we could compute the um  the cumulative distribution function of  the anomaly scores for the anomalies  uh it would look like this then we could  set our threshold tau here to get you  know five percent uh the the five  percent quantile uh of of this uh cdf  instead of the pdf uh  um and so that's what we would like to  do  but of course we don't have the cdf for  the anomaly scores either  but uh but the if the if the uh  densities are our mixture then the  the cdfs are also a mixture  and if the cdf's are mixture then we can  do some little algebra here and we can  solve for the anomaly cdf  by subtracting  the  the mixture and the nominal and dividing  by alpha  so ideally we'd have a picture that  looks like this we estimate f naught and  fm from our two samples s naught and s m  plug them in here and we get out f a  reality unfortunately looks like this at  least for a small sample i chose a  particularly bad looking one but we can  see that our empirical cdfs for for the  anomaly scores for f naught and for fm  are beautiful you know uh empirical cdfs  they're step functions but they but  they're monotonically increasing they  start at zero they end at one  but the but when we subtract them we get  a mess right this is not increasing  monotonically it goes negative it does  start at zero and end at one  but uh but what can we do  um well what we're going to use is  actually  if we're interested in like the the five  percentile here  we will use the rightmost crossing of  this to set our threshold tau because  that's the safe choice  fortunately the real  case looks a bit like this although if  you zoom in you still might have  multiple crossings here but we're going  to use the right most edge  and then we can prove a theorem  in the pac style that says um if we have  this amount of training data then with  probability one minus delta we will have  an alien detection rate of at least one  minus q plus epsilon so epsilon is our  kind of error approximation parameter  and it enters here as a one over epsilon  squared which is the typical thing you  see in uh these kind of concentration  bound based methods  um and alpha also appears here it  basically is one over alpha squared so  as alpha gets tiny and his epsilon gets  tiny our required sample size gets very  large  and this makes sense because when alpha  is very small we actually have very few  anomalies in that mixture distribution  so the sample size needs to get big  enough so that we can actually get  enough anomalies  uh effectively to to measure uh fa  so it uh what about estimating alpha how  can we figure out what alpha is well uh  from a strict uh point of view it is not  identifiable in general  but under reasonable assumptions we can  obtain a pretty good estimate and this  was an experiment where we compared uh  uh five different estimates from the  literature with the ground truth so the  the truth is this uh pink line here and  we can see that this orange line here  which is uh from a paper by patra and  send 2016  is giving a quite decent performance  doesn't do too well in this one letter  recognition data set but it's doing  quite well on the others  so uh my students see you led this work  along with rasheed garpali divashis  mandal and myself and uh and we have a  paper under review  let me wrap up this entire presentation  by reviewing what i covered so first we  talked about the sort of main families  of anomaly detection methods  distance based methods density methods  density quantile estimation and then  reconstruction error  and then we switched and looked at  deep learning from the point of view of  learning a good representation in which  we could use distance or density  estimation or density quantile  estimation  or possibly reconstruction  and uh and we compared basically we  showed the supervised classification  with softmax is already not terrible uh  although there is a lot of headroom for  extracting the anomaly score  better than than just using the logits  we saw that you can gain some  improvements by changing the final layer  to to not be just the standard softmax  we saw that we could also gain  improvements by using a hybrid a  two-headed network where we combine  supervised classification with one of  these anomaly detection losses say  density estimation or distances or  reconstruction  and then the most speculative technique  is instance contrastive learning and uh  and we show that it while it shows some  promise it also appears to show the same  weaknesses as supervised classification  in that the uh anomaly points uh are not  given distinct representations uh at  least compared to the to the nominal  points  so i think there are many open research  questions here this is not by any means  a finished topic  we really need to understand what's  going on in representation learning can  we come up with improved methods that  give us representations that represent  a a space that is uh that goes beyond  just our training data can we  extrapolate the representation  um and then we need methods for  comparing these learned representations  to study them and and uh and i and it  would be particularly wonderful if we  could have an open framework where we  could do control the comparisons of  these methods because as you can see  i couldn't build you just one table  showing the comparison of all these  methods because everyone is using  different setups  and and some are using the ood setting  some are using the uh the open category  setting  and if we can get all that working there  are still many other questions for  instance how can we explain the  anomalies we find to users  or to subject matter experts we said  well this case looks weird figure you  figure out why we want to say here's why  we think it's unusual  and if we can get feedback from users uh  in previous work at oregon state um  by amran siddiki  he showed in his thesis that  incorporating user feedback in online  into the anomaly detection procedure can  be extremely powerful in improving our  true positive rates  and reducing our false alarm rates  and of course if we are discovering new  categories we should be learning  definitions for them say with few shot  learning and incorporating them into the  classifier but few shot learning has  basically the same representational  needs as anomaly detection these new  points should should be meaningfully  separated from the previously known  classes  well i'll stop here and conclude in the  slides i have uh the bibliography for  all the things that i cited here  and i'm eager to uh to hear your  reactions  you can reach me on twitter as at  tdtrick or my email is tgd.cs.orst.edu
 

